    "label": "9",
    "prompt": "Explain why it is not possible to evaluate $(-9)^{\\frac{1}{2}}$ as a real number.",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "$(-9)^{\\frac{1}{2}}=\\sqrt{-9}$ has no real value because no real number squares to a negative number.",
      "worked": "$$(-9)^{\\frac{1}{2}}=\\sqrt{-9}$$\nThe square of every real number is non-negative, so no real number has square $-9$.",
